| Dimension | Transactional Outbox | CDC (Kafka Connect) |
|-----------|----------------------|---------------------|
| **Atomicity** | Guaranteed by a single database transaction that writes both domain data and outbox record. | No single transaction; relies on WAL ordering and eventual consistency. |
| **Latency** | Slightly higher due to an extra write to the outbox table, but bounded by the database commit time. | Near‑zero latency if CDC agent is colocated; otherwise subject to replication lag. |
| **Operational Footprint** | Requires a lightweight outbox table and a periodic poller or trigger‑based publisher. | Requires a CDC connector and a separate Kafka topic for change events. |
| **Schema Evolution** | Outbox schema can evolve independently; events are serialized separately. | CDC captures raw row changes; schema changes may require connector reconfiguration. |
| **Observability** | Queue depth metrics on outbox table; explicit tracing from domain write to event. | CDC lag metrics; tracing via connector instrumentation. |

In practice, the outbox pattern excels when strict transactional guarantees are paramount and the service owns its own event schema. CDC shines in environments where multiple services share a common database and a unified change stream is desired. Both patterns, when coupled with Kafka’s exactly‑once semantics and a robust idempotency layer, provide a resilient foundation for event‑driven microservices.

## 💡 Production Code Implementation & Patterns

Below is a concrete, production‑ready Java implementation that ties together the Outbox and CDC patterns, demonstrates resilience, and showcases clean domain modeling. The code is deliberately minimal yet complete enough to be dropped into a Spring Boot project.

### 1. Low‑Level Domain Model

```java
// Domain event abstraction
public interface DomainEvent {
    UUID getId();
    Instant getTimestamp();
    EventType getType();
}

// Concrete event
public class OrderCreatedEvent implements DomainEvent {
    private final UUID id;
    private final Instant timestamp;
    private final UUID orderId;
    private final BigDecimal amount;

    public OrderCreatedEvent(UUID orderId, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.id = UUID.randomUUID();
        this.timestamp = Instant.now();
        this.orderId = orderId;
        this.amount = amount;
    }

    @Override public UUID getId() { return id; }
    @Override public Instant getTimestamp() { return timestamp; }
    @Override public EventType getType() { return EventType.ORDER_CREATED; }
    public UUID getOrderId() { return orderId; }
    public BigDecimal getAmount() { return amount; }
}

// Outbox persistence entity
@Entity
@Table(name = "outbox")
public class OutboxMessage {
    @Id @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;
    private UUID eventId;
    private String payload;
    private String status; // PENDING, SENT
    // getters/setters omitted
}

// Enum for event kinds
public enum EventType { ORDER_CREATED, ORDER_CANCELLED, PAYMENT_PROCESSED }
```

### 2. Repository & Publisher Contracts

```java
public interface OutboxRepository extends JpaRepository<OutboxMessage, Long> {
    List<OutboxMessage> findByStatus(String status);
}

public interface EventPublisher {
    void publish(DomainEvent event);
}
```

### 3. Resilience‑Aware Kafka Outbox Publisher

```java
@Component
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class KafkaOutboxPublisher implements EventPublisher {

    private final OutboxRepository outboxRepo;
    private final KafkaTemplate<String, String> kafkaTemplate;

    @Retry(name = "outboxRetry")
    @CircuitBreaker(name = "outboxCircuit", fallbackMethod = "fallbackPublish")
    @Override
    public void publish(DomainEvent event) {
        OutboxMessage msg = new OutboxMessage();
        msg.setEventId(event.getId());
        msg.setPayload(serialize(event));
        msg.setStatus("PENDING");
        outboxRepo.save(msg);
    }

    private String serialize(DomainEvent event) {
        // Jackson or Protobuf serialization
        return new ObjectMapper().writeValueAsString(event);
    }

    private void fallbackPublish(DomainEvent event, Throwable t) {
        // Persist to a dead‑letter table or alert
        log.error("Failed to enqueue event {}", event.getId(), t);
    }
}
```

### 4. Spring REST Endpoint

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/orders")
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class OrderController {

    private final OrderService orderService;
    private final EventPublisher publisher;

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<OrderDto> create(@RequestBody OrderDto dto) {
        Order order = orderService.save(dto);
        publisher.publish(new OrderCreatedEvent(order.getId(), order.getAmount()));
        return ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/orders/" + order.getId())).body(order.toDto());
    }
}
```

